---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="About" description="Who is behind Ground Floor and why this project exists.">

  <div class="page-aurora">
    <div class="page-aurora-glow"></div>
    <div class="max-w-wide mx-auto px-5 md:px-10 pt-24 pb-20 relative">
      <p class="font-mono text-[0.7rem] tracking-[0.2em] uppercase mb-4" style="color:#9b5de5;">About</p>
      <h1 class="font-display font-semibold tracking-tight text-chalk"
          style="font-size:clamp(2.4rem,5vw,3.75rem);line-height:1.06;">Ground Floor</h1>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="max-w-prose mx-auto px-5 md:px-0 py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="prose-editorial">

      <p>
        I run this project from a home office. The gear doing the work is two M5 Max
        MacBook Pros connected over Thunderbolt 5 RDMA — 256 GB of unified memory,
        4 TB of storage, ~3 µs latency between nodes. The same hardware I write about
        on this site. None of it is theoretical.
      </p>

      <h2>Why this work</h2>
      <p>
        I kept seeing the same conversation repeat. A solo physician asks about AI
        documentation tools. The products people recommend — all of them excellent —
        require an internet connection, a vendor agreement, and some number of calls
        with a compliance consultant. The physician, who runs a single-provider
        practice with two staff and no IT department, quietly closes the browser tab.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tools that exist for large health systems don't translate well to small
        practices. The tools built for small practices often handle the compliance
        question with a checkbox and a promise. There's a third option that almost
        nobody is covering in a systematic way: run the model locally, on hardware you
        already understand, and don't create a third-party data flow in the first place.
      </p>
      <p>
        That's the gap Ground Floor is trying to close. Not with a product — this is
        an education project, not a company — but with clear documentation of what
        actually works.
      </p>

      <h2>The format</h2>
      <p>
        Each week I run a specific experiment: a specific model, on specific hardware,
        doing a specific task for a specific regulated industry. I document the setup,
        the results, the failure modes, and what someone would need to replicate it.
        The verdict answers the question most people actually have: <em>is this worth my time?</em>
      </p>
      <p>
        The companion videos go on YouTube. The writeups live here. The discussion
        happens on LinkedIn. This site is the reference layer.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'm not</h2>
      <p>
        I'm not a lawyer, a compliance consultant, a physician, a financial advisor,
        or a licensed professional in any of the industries I write about. I'm someone
        who understands local LLM infrastructure and wants to make that knowledge
        accessible to people who don't have time to piece it together from technical
        papers and Discord servers.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every experiment result is a technical finding. The compliance implications
        for your practice are a separate question that requires someone who knows your
        specific situation. The <a href="/scope">Scope &amp; Disclaimers</a> page says this more carefully.
      </p>

      <h2>The lab setup</h2>
      <p>
        Two M5 Max MacBook Pros connected over Thunderbolt 5 RDMA — 256 GB total
        unified memory, 4 TB storage, ~800 GB/s peak bandwidth between nodes.
        This cluster handles the full experiment range: from entry-tier simulations
        (deliberately constrained to 16 GB to match a base Mac mini) up to large-context
        multi-step workloads at 70B.
      </p>
      <p>
        When an experiment is documented as "viable at entry level," the setup was
        deliberately throttled to that tier. The cluster gives room to test across
        the full hardware spectrum without buying multiple machines.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Best way to reach me is on <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/xavier-jones-xajo/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a> or via the <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@XavierOutside" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouTube channel</a>.
        I read everything. If you're a practitioner in a regulated industry who wants
        to share a use case, challenge a finding, or suggest an experiment — especially welcome.
        The experiments get better when they're grounded in real workflows.
      </p>

    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
